notes.body:
I understand, but the excerpt has no OOO while the section you updated is dedicated to OOO pref enabled (it still solves the issue though.. that's not the point)

That's why I suggested, maybe not clearly enough, to update another part some lines later (in order to reuse the LT_SEQ test which was designed for this apparently). In this case, we could rely on `!has_gap` , such as

```
   5056     /* If we are not processing out of order, update the max nextseq value if
   5057      * is later than our current value (or our first value.)
   5058      */
   5059     if ( (!reassemble_ooo || !has_gap) && tcpd && !(tcpd->fwd->flags & TCP_FLOW_REASSEMBLE_UNTIL_FIN)) {
   5060         if (!PINFO_FD_VISITED(pinfo)) {
   5061             if (LT_SEQ(tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq, nxtseq) || tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq == 0) {
   5062                 tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq = nxtseq;
   5063             }
   5064         }
   5065     }
```

What do you think of this, and does it work with the pref enabled for the whole PCAP ?

All right. We need to test when the value is 0 only, and reuse the LT_SEQ test, similar to this:


```
                /* Update the maximum expected seqno if no SYN packet was seen
                 * before, or if the new segment succeeds previous segments. */
                if(tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq == 0) {
                    tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq = nxtseq;
                }
```
. . .
```
    if ((!reassemble_ooo || !has_gap) && tcpd && !(tcpd->fwd->flags & TCP_FLOW_REASSEMBLE_UNTIL_FIN)) {
        if (!PINFO_FD_VISITED(pinfo)) {
            if (LT_SEQ(tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq, nxtseq) || tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq == 0) {
                tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq = nxtseq;
            }
        }
    }
```

changes_data.diff:
@@ -5024,8 +5024,12 @@ again:

             if (!has_gap) {
                 /* Update the maximum expected seqno if no SYN packet was seen
-                 * before, or if the new segment succeeds previous segments. */
-                tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq = nxtseq;
+                 * before, or if the new segment succeeds previous segments.
+                 * Ignore if nxtseq is lower than the current maxnextseq,
+                 * which might happen if we are dealing with an OOO or retransmission.*/
+                if (LT_SEQ(tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq, nxtseq) || tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq == 0) {
+                    tcpd->fwd->maxnextseq = nxtseq;
+                }

                 /* If there is no gap, look for any OOO packets that are now
                  * contiguous. */
